  abstract = {Item response theory (IRT) is the cornerstone of modern measurement. The benefits of using IRT have been well documented. In order to use IRT models, it is necessary to estimate item parameters. As assessments become more complex, more sophisticated models need to be developed. The difficulty in making models more complex is the issue of parameter estimation, and, without the ability to estimate parameters, the models are not useful. With the growth of computer technology, new estimation techniques are being developed for IRT. One such technique is Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC). MCMC provides parameter estimates by simulating draws from the posterior distribution of the parameters and uses the constructed samples to provide point estimates of the parameters (e.g., means and modes). Both Gibbs sampling and Metropolis--Hastings within Gibbs are described, and examples of estimating parameters of dichotomous IRT models are provided.},
