  abstract = {In linear multiple regression, ``enhancement'' is said to occur when R{$^2$}=b{$\prime$}r{$>$}r{$\prime$}r, where b is a p\texttimes 1 vector of standardized regression coefficients and r is a p\texttimes 1 vector of correlations between a criterion y and a set of standardized regressors, x. When p=1 then b{$\equiv$}r and enhancement cannot occur. When p=2, for all full-rank Rxx {$\neq$} I, Rxx =E[xx{$\prime$}]=V {$\Lambda$} V{$\prime$} (where V {$\Lambda$} V{$\prime$} denotes the eigen decomposition of Rxx; {$\lambda_{1}>\lambda_2$}), the set B{$_1$} := \textbraceleft bi : R{$^2$} = b{$\prime$}i ri = r{$\prime$}iri ;0 i : R{$^2$} = b{$\prime$}iri {$>$} r{$\prime$}iri; 0iri {$\lambda_2$} {$>$} {$\cdot\cdot\cdot$} {$>\lambda$}p),, both sets contain an uncountably infinite number of vectors. Geometrical arguments demonstrate that B{$_1$} occurs at the intersection of two hyper-ellipsoids in {$\mathbb{R}$}p . Equations are provided for populating the sets B{$_1$} and B{$_2$} and for demonstrating that maximum enhancement occurs when b is collinear with the eigenvector that is associated with {$\lambda$}p (the smallest eigenvalue of the predictor correlation matrix). These equations are used to illustrate the logic and the underlying geometry of enhancement in population, multiple-regression models. R code for simulating population regression models that exhibit enhancement of any degree and any number of predictors is included in Appendices A and B. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)},
